# Regression test: `apply`-ing a constructor of a *parametrized* inductive
# type/proposition (eg. `And`, `Or`) - about as ordinary as tactic proofs
# get, yet a chain of four separate bugs made it impossible:
#
#  1. A constructor's field types (eg. `left: P -> Or(P, Q)`) are elaborated
#     on their own, before the inductive's own parameters (`P`, `Q`) exist as
#     enclosing binders, so any reference to them inside the field type was
#     elaborated as an unbound/global variable rather than a properly bound
#     one - even after later wrapping the field type in the parameters'
#     Products. This made such a reference structurally indistinguishable
#     from an unrelated global constant, so unification could never solve
#     for it.
#  2. Once (1) was fixed and such a variable *could* be solved for, the
#     unifier's own final substitution-reduction step only knew how to
#     finalize `?`-metavariable solutions, and panicked (`ParseIntError`) on
#     a solved plain named variable.
#  3. `apply` still didn't propagate that solution into the lemma's
#     remaining premises, so eg. applying `left : P -> Or(P, Q)` to prove
#     `Or(Foo, Bar)` asked the next tactic step to prove the literal
#     type-level premises `PROP`/`PROP` (nonsense) instead of narrowing down
#     to the one real premise, `Foo`.
#  4. The occurs-check used while solving for such a variable compared
#     candidates *by display name only*, so it misfired as a bogus
#     self-reference whenever the caller's own axiom happened to share a
#     name with the constructor's parameter (eg. both called `P`) - which is
#     the overwhelmingly common case, since callers naturally reuse a
#     predicate's own parameter names for their instantiations.

inductive Or (P: PROP, Q: PROP) : PROP {
  | left: P -> Or(P, Q)
  | right: Q -> Or(P, Q)
}
inductive And (P: PROP, Q: PROP) : PROP {
  | conj: P -> Q -> And(P, Q)
}

axiom P : PROP;
axiom Q : PROP;
axiom p : P;
axiom q : Q;

theorem or_via_apply : Or(P, Q) :=
  begin
  apply left
  exact p
  qed.

theorem and_via_apply : And(P, Q) :=
  begin
  apply conj
  exact p
  exact q
  qed.
